a reference is an address, not a name — `general/addressability`. inline
at point of use: no footer list, no `[1]` marker. a marker is not an
address, and the jump is its cost.

form: backticked path from the tree root, optional `:/pattern/`.
no leading ./, no extension, no bare name.

  `general/terse`                a leaf
  `general/terse:/the test/`     a place in one
  `src/errors.py:/def resolve/`  code, from the repo root
  https://9p.io/sys/doc          external, already self-addressing

address by content, never by number. a tool emits file:line for output
read once and discarded; a reference outlives the next edit, and a
number does not — it drifts, then points elsewhere without saying so. a
pattern that stops matching is loud, and it fails when the thing cited
is gone. that is the failure you want.

resolution is sam's: first match forward. a pattern need not be unique
to be a function, only short and load-bearing — the words that are the
point, not a line that churns.

  grep -rnoE '`[a-z0-9][a-z0-9._-]*(/[a-z0-9._-]+)+(:/[^/`]+/)?`'

the path separator is the discriminator. backticks also hold code —
`sort`, `column -t` — and code carries none, so a reference is decidable
from syntax alone, before any lookup: extract, then dereference. what
does not open is broken, loudly.

that is what the bare sibling name costs. `terse` from inside general
resolves as purely, against the referring leaf's directory, but is one
shape with `sort`: a typo reads as prose and validation dies. nor are
bare names unique — `dry` is both `ai/skills/dry` and `programming/dry`.

an empty target is fine — unwritten, not absent, and the gap stays
visible. a pattern into one is not.

a root-level leaf has no branch, so no address. nest it.

this leaf shows the form, so its examples are shapes, not claims — the
only leaf a validator skips.

ref: ~/src/sys/bin/ref. `ref -x general/reference` checks this tree. run
it before the doc is done — you are the one who moved the line it
pointed at.
